From a174b73a0b0be559ad1f8aa491fc043d477b736e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Pfister <119864532+JJRPF@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 17:59:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t602x: add the CPU energy model (enables
 EAS on M2 Pro/Max)

T602x has no CPU energy model, so energy-aware scheduling stays off even though
apple-soc-cpufreq already registers the model from the OPP table: the cluster
OPP tables carry no opp-microwatt, while T8103, T600x, T8112, T8132 and T8140
do. Add opp-microwatt to every OPP of both core types.

The values are derived, not measured on T602x: T8112 (M2) has the same cores,
Blizzard and Avalanche, on the same process, and its opp-microwatt was
measured with the SMC cluster power meters. Its per-OPP power is fitted to
P = (a + b f) V^2 f per core type with T6020's voltage for each OPP (from the
ADT's pmgr voltage-states), and the fit gives T6020's OPPs, within 6% rms of
T8112's measurements for both core types. The energy per cycle rises from each
OPP to the next, so no OPP is marked inefficient and cpufreq keeps choosing
among all of them; only the scheduler's placement changes.

Same-boot A/B on a J414s (M2 Pro), headless idle, kernel.sched_energy_aware 1
vs 0, 12 paired rounds of 20 s: system power is 0.230 W lower with the energy
model (lower in 12 of 12; SoC power 0.156 W). All six T602x boards build with
W=1 and no new warnings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RuRMQncGu3ukpyW44vHHjQ
